    "result_text": "The change is complete and the suite is green.\n\n## What I did\n\nThe spec required validating the E.164 national-number length against the country code \u2014 `+61` needs 9 national digits, `+1` needs 10. I found that telephone normalization/validation lives in `Telephones.toE164()` (used by `RequireOwnerFields` on create, which rejects a `null` result as a 400).\n\nPreviously `toE164` only enforced a generic \"8\u201315 total digits\" rule, so a `+61` number with the wrong national-digit count (or a `+1` number that wasn't 10 digits) would incorrectly pass. I added a per-country national-length check in `Telephones.java`:\n\n- A `NATIONAL_LENGTHS` map: `61 \u2192 9`, `1 \u2192 10`.\n- After the generic digit-count check, if the number begins with a recognized country code, the national portion (digits after the code) must match that country's required length, else it returns `null` \u2192 400.\n- Unrecognized country codes (e.g. the `+64` in the javadoc example) still fall back to the generic 8\u201315 rule.\n\nThe two known codes start with different first digits (`61` vs `1`), so there's no ambiguity in matching. Existing valid data stays valid: `uniqueTelephone()` (`04XXXXXXXX` \u2192 `+61` + 9 national digits) still passes.\n\nTest result: the new `errorRejectsWrongNationalLength` (`\"+61 123\"` \u2192 400) passes, and the full build is green (`BUILD SUCCESS`, 1 test, 0 failures).",
